<main>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Article Summary</h2>
    <p class="author">Summary of an article written by Tim Cross-Kovoor of VideoWeek.</p>

    <p class="lead">
      When major agency groups discuss their AI investments, they often emphasise
      the new possibilities for personalisation and creativity at scale that the
      technology makes possible.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, the practical application of AI is currently strongly associated
      with improving operational efficiency and supporting established media
      workflows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Most Common AI-Supported Workflows</h2>

    <div class="insights">
      <div class="insight">
        <strong>01 — Reporting</strong>
        Reporting is identified as the most common AI-supported workflow.
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <strong>02 — Programmatic Optimisation</strong>
        Programmatic optimisation is the next most frequent use case.
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <strong>03 — Media Planning & Strategy</strong>
        AI is also being applied to media planning and strategic workflows.
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <strong>04 — Content Creation</strong>
        Content creation represents another significant area of AI adoption.
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <strong>05 — Source Tracking</strong>
        AI-supported source tracking is among the emerging workflow applications.
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <strong>06 — Dynamic Creative Optimisation</strong>
        Dynamic creative optimisation is also being explored and implemented.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Agentic AI and Human Input</h2>

    <p>
      Agentic AI implementations still generally require a significant amount
      of human input. The technology may automate or coordinate parts of a
      workflow, but human involvement remains an important component of current
      implementations.
    </p>

    <p>
      There are also mixed expectations regarding how quickly agentic systems
      will become a major part of day-to-day workflows.
    </p>

    <div class="highlight">
      <strong>The central tension</strong>
      <p>
        There appears to be a gap between what AI is delivering today and what
        organisations expect it to deliver one year from now.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Looking Ahead</h2>

    <p>
      Given the pace at which agentic AI capabilities are evolving, it is
      understandable that there may be a significant difference between current
      AI performance and expectations for the near future.
    </p>

    <p>
      The discussion therefore highlights an important distinction between
      <strong>current practical AI adoption</strong> and the
      <strong>future potential of increasingly autonomous systems</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

